<!-- SECTION B: A model for the course -->

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">A question before we start</p>

# When did data journalism start?

Take a guess. Most people say sometime in the last fifteen years.

<Notes>

Ask students for guesses out loud before revealing the answer.

</Notes>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Try 1848</p>

# A sitting congressman named Greeley

<Screenshot url="propublica.org" label="ProPublica story: Antebellum Data Journalism">
  <img
    src="{base}/screenshots/social-science-in-a-hurry/propublica-antebellum-data-journalism.png"
    alt="ProPublica headline 'Antebellum Data Journalism: Or, How Big Data Busted Abe Lincoln,' with a portrait of Horace Greeley"
  />
</Screenshot>

In 1848, New York Tribune editor — and sitting congressman — Horace Greeley
used U.S. Post Office route data to calculate how much each member of
Congress should have been paid in mileage reimbursement, then compared it to
what they actually charged. He published the full table. Abraham Lincoln
came in $677 over, about $18,700 today — among the worst.

<Notes>

Source: Scott Klein, "Antebellum Data Journalism: Or, How Big Data Busted Abe
Lincoln," ProPublica (2015):
https://www.propublica.org/nerds/antebellum-data-journalism-busted-abe-lincoln.
Two future vice presidents (Hamlin, Johnson) and other legends of the era
also appear on Greeley's list. Data journalism predates Meyer by more than a
century, and predates FOIA entirely.

</Notes>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Fifty years later, another hero</p>

# W.E.B. Du Bois draws the data

<Screenshot url="loc.gov" label="Du Bois's 1900 chart, Distribution of Negroes in the United States">
  <img
    src="{base}/screenshots/social-science-in-a-hurry/du-bois-georgia-negro.png"
    alt="Du Bois's 1900 hand-drawn map 'Distribution of Negroes in the United States,' color-coded by population density"
  />
</Screenshot>

For the 1900 Paris Exposition, sociologist and journalist W.E.B. Du Bois and
his students at Atlanta University hand-drew dozens of charts and maps —
"The Georgia Negro" series — turning census data into a rigorous, visual
argument about Black life in America.

<Notes>

Du Bois was already a trained empirical sociologist (The Philadelphia Negro,
1899) and would go on to edit The Crisis — bridging social science and
journalism directly. This is the bridge between Greeley's stunt and Meyer's
discipline: Du Bois was doing careful, evidence-based social science and
presenting it for a mass public audience decades before Meyer named it.
Source: Library of Congress, "Du Bois in Paris – Exposition Universelle,
1900," https://blogs.loc.gov/inside_adams/2015/02/du-bois-in-paris-exposition-universelle-1900/.

</Notes>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">A hundred and twenty years later</p>

# Philip Meyer and "social science in a hurry"

> "Journalism must become social science in a hurry." — Philip Meyer

In 1967, after the Detroit riots, Meyer argued that newsrooms needed to
borrow the discipline of social science — survey design, hypothesis
testing — to report accurately under deadline.

<Notes>

Ask students why this quote still holds up today. Point out that data
journalism is reporting first, with analysis as a tool, not the other way
around.

</Notes>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">The Detroit Free Press, 1967</p>

# "People Beyond 12th Street"

After the July 1967 uprising, the conventional wisdom blamed outsiders and
the least educated. The Free Press — working with the Detroit Urban League
and the University of Michigan Survey Research Center — surveyed Black
residents in the riot areas directly. Education didn't cleanly separate
people who said they took part from those who didn't.

<Notes>

Attribute this to the newsroom and its research partners, not Meyer alone.
Name the survey's limits: it measures responses, not a single cause. Keep
the racial and political context visible — don't reduce the uprising to a
data exercise. Sources: University of Michigan Survey Research Center news
item on the 1967 survey; Philip Meyer, "A Newspaper's Role Between the
Riots," Nieman Reports (1968).

</Notes>

</Slide>

<!-- SECTION C: Course framing + the story arc -->

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Tonight's roadmap</p>

# Six weeks, one arc

1. Identifying newsworthy questions
2. Locating data that can provide the answers
3. Preparing raw data for rigorous analysis
4. Interviewing data to develop meaningful findings
5. Verifying your findings in the real world
6. Ensuring subjects have an opportunity to respond

<Notes>

Preview that each of these steps will return in future weeks with hands-on
practice.

</Notes>

</Slide>

