<Slide variant="section">

<p class="slide-eyebrow">A closer look</p>

# The story arc: turn a question into news

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 1 of 9</p>

# Find a newsworthy question

Every data story starts as a question someone cares about — not a
spreadsheet.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 2 of 9</p>

# Work out how to measure it

Decide what a newsworthy answer would look like, and how you'd know it when
you saw it.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 3 of 9</p>

# Test the hypothesis

Find out whether the answer is true — like science, but in a hurry.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 4 of 9</p>

# Adopt a database frame of mind

Identify the data you'll need to gather, acquire, or create to answer the
question.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 5 of 9</p>

# Get or make the data

Download it from a city portal, file a FOIA request, scrape the web,
digitize public records, or observe it yourself.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 6 of 9</p>

# Interview the data

Treat it like a human source, plus the technical questions that are unique
to data.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 7 of 9</p>

# Ask and answer questions

Use spreadsheets, databases, and code — sorts, filters, aggregations,
searches.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 8 of 9</p>

# Ground-truth the findings

Check your results against the real world, other sources, and outside
standards.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 9 of 9</p>

# Find the people and places

Bring the findings back to the real people the story is about.

</Slide>

<!-- SECTION D: Run the model twice -->

<Slide variant="section">

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Same nine steps, twice</p>

# Run the model twice

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 1 of 9</p>

# Find a newsworthy question

After the 1967 uprising, what happened — and did the popular explanations
fit the evidence?

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 2 of 9</p>

# Work out how to measure it

Survey residents in the affected neighborhoods; compare responses across
groups, including education.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 3 of 9</p>

# Test the hypothesis

Challenge the assumption that participants were mostly outsiders or the
least educated — the survey didn't support a simple education-based story.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 4 of 9</p>

# Adopt a database frame of mind

Treat each completed questionnaire as a record, and each survey question or
respondent characteristic as a field.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 5 of 9</p>

# Get or make the data

The team created survey data — interviews designed and conducted with the
Detroit Urban League and the University of Michigan Survey Research Center.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 6 of 9</p>

# Interview the data

Who designed and administered the survey? Who was reachable? How was
nonresponse handled?

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 7 of 9</p>

# Ask and answer questions

Compare response patterns across groups, rank reported grievances, and
present results in charts and maps.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 8 of 9</p>

# Ground-truth the findings

Check the survey's claims against other reporting and the lived conditions
of the neighborhoods.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Detroit, step 9 of 9</p>

# Find the people and places

Return to residents and neighborhoods — keep the racial and political
context visible.

</Slide>

